-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Aliasing:** If the rendering target resolution does not meet the Nyquist criterion, ghosts or non-existent frequencies appear [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Ghosting/Copy Artifacts:** Caused by the absence of zero-padding in the spatial domain during pre-processing, resulting in visible copies of the dataset [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Vignetting Artifact:** Central values of the final image are overemphasized due to the frequency response of the interpolation filter. This can be suppressed by spatial premultiplication in pre-processing, though this may increase ghosting [S2, unofficial].
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Aliasing:** The rendering target resolution must meet the Nyquist criterion. Low resampling quality introduces ghosts or non-existent frequencies [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Ghosting/Copy Artifacts:** Caused by the absence of zero-padding in the spatial domain, resulting in visible copies of the dataset [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Vignetting Artifact:** Central values of the final image are overemphasized due to the frequency response of the interpolation filter. Mitigated by spatial premultiplication with the reciprocal of the filter's frequency response [S2, unofficial].
 *   **Overlapping Artifacts:** Occur if the projection slice resolution is insufficient for the dataset resolution (e.g., a $256^3$ dataset requires at least a $512 \times 512$ slice) [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Hardware Limitations:** NVIDIA GPUs do not natively support floating-point 3D textures; values must be stored in four-channel fixed-point textures, requiring additional conversion before interpolation [S1, unofficial].
